1. Overview
The Circle Management module is designed to allow users to create, update, view, approve, and manage Circle-related information in a structured and controlled workflow.
The module ensures that:
All new circle creations and updates go through a request process.
Every action is tracked with a Request ID and status.
Approvals and rejections are handled by authorized users.
Transparency is maintained across all user activities.
2. Accessing Circle Management
After logging into the system:
Navigate to Circle Management from the main menu.
You will see two primary options:
Circle Management
Circle Request
🔵 SECTION 1: Circle Management
When the user clicks on Circle Management, two tabs will be displayed:
Manage Circle
My Requests
3. Manage Circle Tab
This tab displays all approved (accepted) Circle records in a table format.
3.1 Table Columns
The table includes the following columns:
Circle Code
Circle Name
Zone
Action
3.2 Actions Available
In the Action column, users can:
Edit – Modify Circle Name and Zone for an existing circle.
Note: Circle Code is typically non-editable to maintain data integrity.
4. Creating a New Circle
4.1 Steps to Create a Circle
Click on the Create button.
A form will open.
Fill in all required fields:
Circle Code
Circle Name
Zone
Ensure all mandatory fields are completed.
Click Submit / Add.
4.2 After Submission
The request is not directly added to the Manage Circle table.
It is sent for approval.
The newly created request will appear in the My Requests tab.
The request will have a status such as:
Pending
Approved
Rejected
Cancelled
5. Editing an Existing Circle
5.1 Steps to Update a Circle
Go to the Manage Circle tab.
Locate the circle record.
Click Edit in the Action column.
Modify the required fields (Circle Name / Zone).
Click Update.
5.2 After Update
The update is treated as a new request.
The modification request appears in the My Requests tab.
It must be approved before changes reflect in the main Manage Circle list.
🟢 My Requests Tab
The My Requests tab displays all requests submitted by the logged-in user.
6.1 Table Columns
The table includes:
Request ID
Circle Code
Circle Name
Zone
Request Type (Create / Update)
Date of Submission
Status
Action
6.2 Actions in My Requests
Users can:
View – See full details of the request.
Cancel – Cancel a pending request.
Note: Once approved or rejected, a request cannot be cancelled.
🟠 SECTION 2: Circle Request
This section is used by authorized users (approvers).
It allows users to review and take action on requests submitted by other users.
7. Circle Request Screen
In this section, users can:
View all circle creation and update requests.
Review request details.
Approve or Reject requests.
7.1 Table Columns
Request ID
Circle Code
Circle Name
Zone
Request Type
Submitted By
Date of Submission
Status
Action
8. Approving or Rejecting Requests
8.1 Steps to Approve
Open Circle Request.
Locate the request.
Click View to check details.
Click Approve.
Confirm the action.
Result:
The circle will be added or updated in the Manage Circle tab.
Status will change to Approved.
8.2 Steps to Reject
Open the request.
Review details.
Click Reject.
Provide a reason (if required).
Confirm.
Result:
Status will change to Rejected.
Changes will not be applied to the main data.
🔄 Workflow Summary
Create Flow
User → Create Circle → Goes to My Requests → Approver Reviews → Approve → Appears in Manage Circle
Update Flow
User → Edit Circle → Goes to My Requests → Approver Reviews → Approve → Updated in Manage Circle
📌 Important Notes
Direct modification of circle data is not allowed without approval.
Every request is assigned a unique Request ID.
Users can only cancel their own pending requests.
Only authorized users can approve or reject requests.
Approved requests automatically reflect in the Manage Circle tab.
✅ Conclusion
The Circle Management module ensures:
Proper validation and data control
Transparent approval workflow
Full tracking of create and update activities
Secure and role-based access
This structured process maintains accuracy, accountability, and operational efficiency within the system.
